    "created_at": "2021-02-19T00:54:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-02-19T00:54:52Z",
    "body": "It seems like you want to propagate attributes themselves, as opposed to just styling based on an ancestor's attributes, but I don't see the use case requiring that. I could easily be misunderstanding something, but it looks to me like (1) In the normal DOM, you just need to write your CSS to apply to _the descendants of_ the element with the `invalid` attribute, instead of just applying directly to that element, and (2) in the shadow DOM you can write your CSS based on the `invalid` attribute of the host, using a :host-context selector. \r\n\r\nMaybe there's a use case that actually needs the attributes themselves to be inherited, either within CSS or elsewhere, and I'm just not seeing it.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
