    "created_at": "2021-02-19T12:40:48Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-02-19T13:35:34Z",
    "body": "Webkit guys opposed to `:host-context`, and rightfully so, since it's a violation of CSS. It's the same as parent selector, and you cannot have parent selectors in CSS, since, as the name suggests, it's a _cascading_ style sheets. Ignoring almost half of mobile users and a good chunk of desktop users is not an option for a developer. So, for Shadow DOM, it'd be a useful feature.\r\n\r\nAs for normal DOM, we kind of already have it. For example have a look at (disabled attr):\r\n\r\n- https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/fieldset#attributes\r\n- https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Element/optgroup#attributes\r\n\r\nWhich works in a strange way, the form controls inside the form are disabled, but none of the controls have neither attribute set nor property set to `true`.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
